content.text:
Implement the Gitea issue #314:

“feat(frontend): redesign homepage with branded motion and Three.js hero”

Before changing any code, inspect the repository carefully and understand the current implementation.

IMPORTANT PROJECT RULES

This is a multi-developer repository. Do not overwrite, revert, reformat or “clean up” unrelated work. Do not modify files outside the scope of this issue unless genuinely required. Preserve concurrent changes.

Follow the repository’s documented Git methodology, architecture, testing standards, frontend conventions and AI policy.

Read the relevant project documentation before implementation, especially the current versions of:

- docs/design/brand-guidelines.md
- docs/design/frontend-component-baseline.md
- docs/design/information-architecture-and-wireframes.md
- docs/architecture/system-architecture.md where relevant
- docs/development/testing.md
- docs/ai/usage.md or the repository’s equivalent AI documentation
- root/frontend README files containing development and AI instructions

Treat these documents as authoritative.

Do not silently change an approved design decision.

If the repository structure or documentation paths have changed, find the current equivalents rather than creating duplicate documentation.

GOAL

Redesign the public Stat’sTheGame homepage into a distinctive, performant T20 cricket analytics landing page.

The experience should communicate:

“The game, measured ball by ball.”

and visually reinforce the core product idea:

individual cricket event
→ derived statistic
→ traceable source

The result should feel like modern cricket broadcast graphics combined with a serious analytics product.

It must NOT feel like:

- a generic SaaS landing page;
- a gaming/esports interface;
- a betting website;
- an over-animated marketing site.

BRAND

Remain completely within the approved Stat’sTheGame brand guidelines.

Preserve:

- Stat’sTheGame naming;
- approved logo artwork;
- orange apostrophe-bat;
- lime ball/motion-trail language;
- Day Match;
- Night Match;
- semantic colour tokens;
- scoreboard-inspired typography;
- crease-line visual device;
- strong numeric hierarchy;
- event-first visualisation;
- restrained, fast motion.

Use the approved font roles:

- Barlow Condensed for display headings and major numeric values;
- IBM Plex Sans for normal interface/body content;
- IBM Plex Mono only where monospaced technical information has meaning.

Do not regenerate, redraw or replace the approved logo.

Do not introduce arbitrary new brand colours.

Use existing semantic design tokens rather than hard-coded theme-specific colours wherever possible.

HOMEPAGE CONTENT

Build a coherent page with roughly the following narrative.

1. HERO

Use the approved headline:

“The game, measured ball by ball.”

Add concise supporting copy explaining that Stat’sTheGame lets users explore T20 cricket through the events behind each statistic.

Primary CTA:
Browse fixtures

Secondary CTA:
Explore competitions

Use existing route names and components. Confirm routes in the repository before linking.

A player/participant browse link may be included at lower emphasis if consistent with the existing information architecture.

2. THREE.JS HERO

Add one lightweight Three.js visualisation as progressive enhancement.

The scene should be procedural rather than asset-heavy.

Concept:

- simplified cricket pitch/crease;
- cricket ball;
- lime motion trail;
- delivery/event marker;
- a small visual transition showing the event contributing to derived values;
- restrained orange/cyan highlights where permitted by the brand.

The scene should communicate the relationship between a delivery and derived statistics.

IMPORTANT DATA CONSTRAINT

The existing database/source data does NOT contain real ball coordinates, pitch locations, bounce coordinates, shot directions or complete physical trajectories.

Do not fabricate or store such data.

Do not modify the backend/database/schema to support this visual.

Any ball movement in the hero is illustrative.

Do not label an illustrative trajectory as an actual delivery path.

No backend or API changes should be required by this issue.

3. BRAND PRINCIPLES

Present the ideas:

Explosive
Exact
Traceable

Do not implement them as three generic identical SaaS feature cards unless that genuinely produces the strongest design.

Prefer a strong editorial/broadcast layout.

Explosive:
communicate fast event-by-event T20 action.

Exact:
communicate that statistics are derived from underlying events.

Traceable:
communicate that a published statistic can be connected back to event/source provenance.

4. EVENT → STATISTIC STORY

Create a clear visual demonstrating how one delivery affects derived values.

Use only fields/statistics actually supported by the application.

Do not fabricate cricket data capabilities.

Prefer semantic HTML/CSS/SVG for this section instead of additional Three.js canvases.

5. EXPLORE

Provide strong navigation into the existing public application.

Use the current information architecture.

Prominently expose appropriate existing public journeys such as:

- Fixtures
- Competitions
- Players/Participants

Confirm actual route names and terminology from the source before implementing.

6. API / TECHNICAL IDENTITY

Include a restrained section showing that the API/data platform is a first-class product.

Only show endpoint paths that actually exist in the current API/documentation.

Do not invent example endpoints.

Use IBM Plex Mono only for real technical values/paths.

Keep this understandable to non-developers.

7. FINAL CTA

Finish with a simple strong CTA back into public browsing.

Suggested copy direction:

“Follow the game from the first ball.”

Use existing Button/link patterns.

MOTION

Follow the approved motion principle:

“Sharp acceleration, controlled stop.”

Use existing motion variables/easing where available.

Prefer approximately the existing brand timing ranges instead of introducing slow animation.

Motion must communicate one of:

- event;
- transition;
- relationship;
- interaction;
- emphasis.

Avoid decorative animation for its own sake.

Do NOT:

- animate every statistic;
- introduce a long splash/loading animation;
- block interaction while animation completes;
- heavily stagger the whole page;
- make cards dramatically move on hover;
- add permanent neon glow;
- add excessive parallax;
- create animation that makes content harder to read.

THREE.JS PERFORMANCE

Performance is an acceptance requirement, not optional polish.

The page must render meaningful content immediately WITHOUT waiting for Three.js.

Build a complete static hero fallback first.

Then dynamically import/lazy-load the Three.js scene.

Do not put Three.js unnecessarily into the critical initial bundle.

Version one should use procedural geometry.

Do not add:

- large GLB/GLTF stadiums;
- player character models;
- HDR environments;
- huge textures;
- large video backgrounds.

Keep geometry/material count low.

Avoid expensive post-processing.

Avoid unnecessary dynamic shadows.

Clamp device pixel ratio to a reasonable value where required for GPU performance.

Use one Three.js canvas for the homepage hero rather than several independent canvases.

THREE.JS LIFECYCLE

The scene must not waste CPU/GPU continuously.

Implement appropriate lifecycle handling.

Pause/stop/reduce animation when:

- the hero is outside the viewport;
- the page/tab becomes hidden;
- reduced motion is enabled;
- nothing is currently animating, where practical.

IntersectionObserver and document visibility APIs are appropriate if consistent with existing browser support.

Cancel requestAnimationFrame loops on cleanup.

Dispose of geometries, materials, textures and renderer resources correctly when the component unmounts.

Ensure React development/StrictMode behaviour does not create duplicate animation loops/listeners/renderers.

FALLBACK

Three.js is progressive enhancement.

The homepage must still look intentional and complete if:

- Three.js has not loaded yet;
- JavaScript chunk loading fails;
- WebGL is unavailable;
- reduced motion is enabled.

Use a lightweight CSS/SVG/static visual fallback.

Do not show a broken blank canvas.

RESPONSIVENESS

Desktop may use a split hero:

copy | visual

Mobile should deliberately reflow.

Do not simply scale the desktop canvas down.

On mobile:

- reduce visual complexity if useful;
- remove unnecessary pointer-following effects;
- ensure the canvas never blocks vertical scrolling;
- preserve touch targets;
- preserve readable text;
- prevent page-level horizontal overflow.

Test narrow widths supported by the repository.

ACCESSIBILITY

The Three.js canvas must not contain information that is unavailable elsewhere.

All essential messages and navigation must remain semantic HTML.

Canvas animation should be decorative/supportive from an accessibility perspective.

Preserve:

- one meaningful page H1;
- correct heading hierarchy;
- native links/buttons;
- visible keyboard focus;
- sufficient contrast;
- keyboard navigation;
- appropriate touch target sizing;
- screen-reader understandable content.

Do not communicate meaning by colour alone.

REDUCED MOTION

Implement and test prefers-reduced-motion.

When reduced motion is active:

- stop automatic ball travel;
- stop camera movement;
- stop particles;
- stop scroll-driven movement;
- avoid unnecessary transitions.

Render a useful static state.

Do not hide content.

THEMES

Test both:

Day Match
Night Match

The visual hierarchy and functionality must remain the same.

Three.js materials/scene colours should update correctly if the theme changes while the component is mounted.

Do not require a page refresh to make the hero visually correct after an existing theme switch if the current application supports live theme switching.

ARCHITECTURE

Keep Three.js implementation isolated from general page components.

Follow existing repository feature/component conventions.

A possible structure is:

features/home/
  HomePage
  HomeHero
  HeroScene
  HeroSceneFallback
  BrandPrinciples
  EventDerivationStory
  ExploreGateway
  ApiFeature
  HomeCallToAction

This is a suggestion, not an instruction to create a parallel architecture.

Prefer existing components such as Button/Card/PageLayout/etc. where appropriate.

Do not create visually equivalent duplicate primitives when a shared component already exists.

Do not force PageLayout if the approved landing-page structure intentionally differs; inspect the existing homepage/wireframe and make the smallest justified architectural choice.

STATIC HOMEPAGE

The current landing-page design requires no application-data fetch.

Preserve that property.

Do not add API requests to load random fixtures/stats solely for visual decoration.

If illustrative values are needed for explanatory graphics, clearly treat them as explanatory/static examples and do not imply that they are live data. Prefer generic event notation over fabricated named fixtures.

DEPENDENCIES

Inspect the current dependency tree first.

If Three.js is not already installed, add the minimum appropriate dependency through the repository’s existing package-management process.

Do not add multiple animation libraries unless there is a strong documented need.

Prefer CSS/Web Animations/existing utilities for normal UI motion.

Three.js should be used because 3D adds value to the hero, not as the general page-animation system.

Document and motivate new third-party code according to project requirements.

IMPLEMENTATION PROCESS

Work incrementally.

First inspect:

- current Home/Landing implementation;
- styles and semantic tokens;
- theme implementation;
- reusable components;
- routing;
- existing frontend tests;
- Playwright tests;
- accessibility conventions;
- package/build configuration.

Then implement in this order:

1. semantic homepage structure;
2. responsive layout and final content;
3. normal CSS/SVG motion;
4. reduced-motion support;
5. static hero fallback;
6. lazy Three.js scene;
7. lifecycle/performance controls;
8. tests;
9. documentation;
10. performance review.

Do not begin by creating a complex Three.js scene and then retrofit the page around it.

TESTING

Add focused automated coverage consistent with the repository.

At minimum verify:

- headline;
- primary CTA;
- secondary CTA;
- links use correct existing routes;
- important homepage content;
- both theme presentations where the test architecture supports this;
- reduced-motion behaviour;
- fallback behaviour;
- no regression to public browsing.

Add/update Playwright coverage for appropriate end-to-end cases.

Check:

- desktop;
- mobile;
- both themes;
- keyboard navigation;
- Axe/accessibility;
- 200% browser zoom/reflow;
- overflow;
- reduced motion;
- WebGL fallback if practical.

Run the existing relevant frontend tests as well.

Do not weaken/delete existing tests merely to make the implementation pass.

PERFORMANCE

Measure performance before and after where practical.

Inspect production bundle output.

Confirm that Three.js is code-split/lazy-loaded.

Check for obvious layout shift.

Confirm that the page does not perform unnecessary application API calls.

Check scene cleanup and animation lifecycle manually/in tests where appropriate.

Use browser/Lighthouse tooling where available.

If a proposed visual effect causes a material performance regression, simplify the effect rather than accepting the regression.

DOCUMENTATION

Update relevant documentation.

Document:

- homepage design intent;
- use/motivation of Three.js;
- why the visual is illustrative rather than real ball tracking;
- lazy loading/progressive enhancement;
- reduced motion;
- fallback;
- performance considerations;
- testing.

Do not create redundant docs if an existing frontend/design/testing document is the correct place.

SCOPE

Do not modify:

- database schema;
- event model;
- backend API;
- authentication;
- submission workflows;
- admin workflows;
- unrelated public pages;
- approved logo artwork;
- approved brand foundations.

Do not introduce ball-location fields.

Do not introduce unrelated feature work.

Do not refactor unrelated files simply because you notice improvements.

MULTI-DEVELOPER SAFETY

Before editing, inspect git status.

Preserve unrelated working-tree changes.

Do not reset, checkout or discard another developer’s changes.

Do not broadly reformat unrelated files.

Keep the diff tightly scoped.

If current repository changes overlap this issue, integrate carefully rather than replacing newer work with assumptions from this prompt.

QUALITY BAR

The final result should look deliberately designed at:

- desktop;
- tablet;
- mobile;
