The new Three.js homepage hero initially works, but after a few seconds the canvas fails and displays a sad-face/error state.

Debug and fix this as a runtime stability bug.

Do not redesign the homepage and do not add new visual features.

Start by reproducing the issue in the browser with DevTools open and identify the exact console/runtime error.

Pay particular attention to the React/Three.js lifecycle implementation in the hero scene.

Check for:

1. requestAnimationFrame loops continuing after component cleanup;
2. multiple RAF loops being created after rerenders;
3. multiple WebGLRenderer instances being created;
4. React StrictMode mount → cleanup → remount behaviour;
5. renderer.dispose() being called while callbacks can still render;
6. renderer.forceContextLoss() being used unnecessarily;
7. stale IntersectionObserver callbacks;
8. stale document visibilitychange listeners;
9. theme changes recreating the whole Three.js scene unnecessarily;
10. geometry/material/renderer resources being recreated on every render;
11. animation callbacks referencing disposed scene/camera/renderer objects;
12. WebGL context loss or excessive GPU/resource allocation.

The Three.js renderer and animation lifecycle must be robust under React StrictMode.

Requirements for the fix:

- Create a single renderer per mounted HeroScene instance.
- Keep Three.js setup inside an effect with a well-defined lifecycle.
- Track the current RAF request ID.
- Never schedule more than one animation loop.
- Cancel the active RAF before disposing resources.
- Disconnect IntersectionObserver before disposal.
- Remove visibility/theme/pointer listeners before disposal.
- Ensure no callback can restart rendering after the component has begun cleanup.
- Dispose geometries/materials and the renderer exactly once.
- Do NOT call renderer.forceContextLoss() during normal React cleanup unless there is a documented reason; ordinary renderer.dispose() should normally be sufficient.
- Do not recreate the renderer merely because the Day Match/Night Match theme changes. Update existing materials/scene properties where practical.
- Guard asynchronous Three.js initialization so that if the component unmounts before initialization finishes, the completed import does not create a renderer afterward.
- Make development StrictMode safe.
- Preserve the static hero fallback if WebGL genuinely fails.

Add defensive handling for:

webglcontextlost
webglcontextrestored

If context loss occurs:
- prevent uncontrolled exception loops;
- stop the RAF;
- show the existing static fallback;
- do not leave a broken/sad canvas visible.

Do not hide the real bug merely by catching all errors.

After fixing, verify:

- leave homepage open for at least 2 minutes;
- scroll hero off-screen and back repeatedly;
- switch Day Match/Night Match repeatedly;
- switch browser tabs and return;
- navigate away and back repeatedly;
- resize desktop/mobile;
- test React development StrictMode;
- check browser console contains no Three.js/WebGL errors;
- confirm only one WebGL canvas/renderer exists for the hero;
- confirm animation does not progressively increase CPU/GPU use;
- confirm reduced-motion still works;
- run the relevant frontend tests and production build.

Report:
- exact root cause;
- exact files changed;
- why the failure appeared only after a few seconds;
- how lifecycle cleanup now works;
- verification performed.

Do not commit the fix yet.

